            "[[childhood-absence-epilepsy|Childhood absence epilepsy]] is one of the idiopathic generalized epilepsy syndromes defined by the ILAE. A previously healthy school-age child has frequent [[absence-seizure|absence seizures]]: brief staring spells lasting seconds, often many times a day, sometimes with eyelid flutter or small automatisms. They start and stop abruptly, and the child carries on as if nothing happened, with no postictal confusion. Teachers may report daydreaming or falling school performance. Hyperventilation for a few minutes in clinic often provokes one. The EEG shows generalized spike-and-wave at about 3 per second (3 Hz). Development and examination are normal. Staring with a warning, automatisms, and confusion afterward suggests focal impaired awareness seizures instead.\n\n[[infantile-spasms|Infantile spasms]] (named infantile epileptic spasms syndrome in the ILAE 2022 classification of syndromes in neonates and infants) begin in infancy, usually in the first year. The spasms are brief, sudden flexion or extension of the neck, trunk, and limbs, lasting a second or two, typically in clusters and often on waking. Parents may think it is colic, reflux, or a startle. Development often stalls or regresses. The classic EEG pattern is [[hypsarrhythmia|hypsarrhythmia]]. Causes include structural brain injury, genetic disorders, and [[tuberous-sclerosis-complex|tuberous sclerosis complex]], and in some no cause is found.\n\nInfantile spasms are an emergency: delay in diagnosis and treatment is linked to worse developmental outcome. A baby with possible spasms needs an urgent EEG and same-day or next-day specialist review. A phone video of an episode helps greatly. Treatment is specialist-led (hormonal therapy or vigabatrin), so the generalist's job is to recognize the pattern and not to be reassured by a normal examination between clusters.",
